---
layout: base.njk
description: Brrrty builds your 11ty site in Rust and writes the same files 11ty 3.1.8 writes, byte for byte, in about a third of the time.
---
{%- comment -%} The latest release, read from the changelog page at build time. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign changelog = collections.all | where: "url", "/changelog/" | first -%}
{%- assign release = changelog.templateContent | split: "<h2>" | slice: 1 | first | split: " - " | first -%}
<div class="home">
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="claim">
    <h1 id="claim">Your 11ty site, built in&nbsp;Rust. <span>Same bytes, in about a third of the&nbsp;time.</span></h1>
    <div class="hero-side">
      <p class="lede">Brrrty is a static site generator that builds an existing 11ty project and writes the same files 11ty&nbsp;3.1.8 writes, byte for byte. Templates, Markdown and data stay where they are. The config moves from JavaScript to Rhai.</p>
      <div class="code install">
<pre><code>npm i -D brrrty</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="install-note">Then <code>"build": "brrrty build"</code> in your <code>package.json</code> scripts. On macOS and Linux. Or, with Rust 1.89 or later, <code>cargo install brrrty</code>.</p>
      <p class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/docs/getting-started/">Move a site over</a>
        <a class="button button-quiet" href="/docs/differences/">What's different from 11ty</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <figure class="bench" aria-labelledby="bench-title">
    <div class="bench-head">
      <h2 id="bench-title">Clean build of a 2,272-file blog</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="runs">
      <li class="run">
        <h3 class="run-env">Apple M4 laptop</h3>
        <div class="run-bars">
          <p class="bar-row is-brrrty" style="--t: 0.85"><span class="who">Brrrty</span><span class="track"><span class="bar"></span><span class="time">0.85 s</span></span></p>
          <p class="bar-row" style="--t: 2.32"><span class="who">11ty 3.1.8</span><span class="track"><span class="bar"></span><span class="time">2.32 s</span></span></p>
        </div>
        <p class="run-x"><span class="x">2.7&times;</span> faster</p>
      </li>
      <li class="run">
        <h3 class="run-env"><code>node:24-alpine</code> CI container</h3>
        <div class="run-bars">
          <p class="bar-row is-brrrty" style="--t: 0.86"><span class="who">Brrrty</span><span class="track"><span class="bar"></span><span class="time">0.86 s</span></span></p>
          <p class="bar-row" style="--t: 3.07"><span class="who">11ty 3.1.8</span><span class="track"><span class="bar"></span><span class="time">3.07 s</span></span></p>
        </div>
        <p class="run-x"><span class="x">3.6&times;</span> faster</p>
      </li>
      <li class="run">
        <h3 class="run-env">GitLab shared runner</h3>
        <div class="run-bars">
          <p class="bar-row is-brrrty" style="--t: 1.47"><span class="who">Brrrty</span><span class="track"><span class="bar"></span><span class="time">1.47 s</span></span></p>
          <p class="bar-row" style="--t: 3.82"><span class="who">11ty 3.1.8</span><span class="track"><span class="bar"></span><span class="time">3.82 s</span></span></p>
        </div>
        <p class="run-x"><span class="x">2.6&times;</span> faster</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <div class="axis" aria-hidden="true"><span style="--i: 0">0</span><span style="--i: 1">1</span><span style="--i: 2">2</span><span style="--i: 3">3</span><span style="--i: 4">4 s</span></div>
    <figcaption>
      <p class="match"><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 8.5l3 3 7-7"/></svg> Output identical: 2,272 of 2,272 files, compared byte for byte on the GitLab runner.</p>
      <p>Bars are drawn to scale. On first load, each one grows for as long as its build took.</p>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="port">
    <div class="band-text">
      <h2 id="port">Port the config. Keep the rest.</h2>
      <p>11ty is configured in JavaScript, which Brrrty doesn't run. The same calls go in <code>brrrty.config.rhai</code>, a small <a href="https://rhai.rs">Rhai</a> script: 11ty's method names in snake_case, with <code>config</code> for <code>eleventyConfig</code>. Your templates, Markdown and data don't change.</p>
      <p><a class="more" href="/docs/getting-started/#1-port-the-config">Porting a config, step by step</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="port">
      <div class="code">
        <p class="code-head">eleventy.config.js</p>
<pre><code>module.exports = function (eleventyConfig) {
  eleventyConfig.addPassthroughCopy("src/images");
  eleventyConfig.addFilter("shout", (s) =&gt; s.toUpperCase() + "!");
  eleventyConfig.addCollection("posts", (api) =&gt;
    api.getFilteredByGlob("./src/posts/*.md").reverse()
  );
  return { dir: { input: "src" } };
};</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="code is-brrrty">
        <p class="code-head">brrrty.config.rhai</p>
<pre><code>config.add_passthrough_copy("src/images");
config.add_filter("shout", |s| s.to_upper() + "!");
config.add_collection("posts", |api|
    api.get_filtered_by_glob("./src/posts/*.md").reverse());

#{ dir: #{ input: "src" } }</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="does">
    <div class="band-text">
      <h2 id="does">What it does</h2>
      <p>Version {{ release }}, and early. Try it on a copy of your site and compare the output with 11ty's before you rely on it.</p>
    </div>
    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>Output</dt><dd>The same files 11ty 3.1.8 writes, byte for byte. It is checked by building test sites with both and comparing every file.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Templates</dt><dd>Liquid, Nunjucks, Markdown and HTML in any mix, with 11ty's data cascade, layouts, pagination, permalinks and collections. <a href="/docs/templates/">Templates</a></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Config</dt><dd><code>brrrty.config.rhai</code>, shaped like <code>eleventyConfig</code>: filters, collections, global data, passthrough copy, and the RSS and syntax highlighting plugins. <a href="/docs/configuration/">Reference</a></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Refusals</dt><dd>Where it can't match 11ty exactly, it refuses that page, says why, and builds the rest. It never writes a page that might differ. <a href="/docs/differences/">What it refuses</a></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Dev server</dt><dd><code>brrrty serve</code> rebuilds on every change and updates open pages in place. <a href="/docs/serve/">Details</a></dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-small" aria-labelledby="name">
    <h2 id="name">The name</h2>
    <div class="band-body">
      <p>Brrr + Eleventy = Brrrty. It's also a nod to Bertie, who holds the Guinness World Record for the fastest tortoise.</p>
      <p>This site is built with Brrrty, from the same repository, on every change.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-small" aria-labelledby="built">
    <h2 id="built">How it was built</h2>
    <div class="band-body">
      <p>Brrrty went from a written spec to running a real 2,272-file blog in about 33 hours, built by Josh Sherman with <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a>. Claude wrote the code and Josh steered. Every change got an independent review and a file-by-file comparison against real 11ty output before it merged. Total model cost: $765.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

